From 9a5efc1dedc4ec41933c039bed7f9fa27e0f7db0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 16:41:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 36/37] fix(x86_64): give virtio all 24 IOAPIC pins, not the 11
 legacy ISA ones

Boks' own `shell` image cannot boot on Windows: the erofs snapshotter gives
each of the image's 7 layers its own virtio-blk device, and with the ext4
writable layer, virtiofs, console, vsock and the NIC that is 12 MMIO devices
against 11 usable interrupt lines. The build fails with

    RegisterBlockDevice(IrqsExhausted)

surfacing through the C API as -22. IRQ_BASE=5/IRQ_MAX=15 is the legacy ISA
range, but an IOAPIC has 24 inputs and this VMM has no other claimant for the
top 8: there is no PCI bus for pins 16-23 to collide with (on real hardware
they carry PIRQ A-H) and no ACPI at all, so no SCI either. Below 5 the pins
are genuinely taken -- PIT, i8042, 8259 cascade and the four 16550s -- which
is why IRQ_BASE does not move.

Raising IRQ_MAX alone would not have worked, and that is the substance of
this change. The guest learns its virtio interrupts from
`virtio_mmio.device=<size>@<addr>:<irq>`, but it only has an irq_desc to hang
them on if the interrupt controller told it the pin exists. We ship no ACPI,
so the MP table is the only description the guest gets, and it declared
mp_INT sources for pins 0-15 only. Linux walks every pin the version register
advertises (`io_apic_get_redir_entries()` -> `nr_registers` ->
`for_each_ioapic_pin`) and in `setup_IO_APIC_irqs()` allocates an IRQ only
for a pin `find_irq_entry()` finds an entry for; the rest are logged "not
connected" and left unmapped. Pins 16-23 were therefore visible but dead, and
a device placed there would have failed at request_irq() with a far more
confusing symptom than IrqsExhausted. So emit one source entry per pin.

Declaring 16-23 as ISA sources is safe on x86_64: `pin_2_irq()` ignores
srcbusirq entirely there (the PIRQ override that reads it is CONFIG_X86_32),
and `alloc_irq_from_domain()` returns irq == gsi == pin for gsi >=
nr_legacy_irqs(), so pin 16 is IRQ 16 to the guest -- exactly what the
command line says. The table grows by 8 entries (64 bytes) inside a region
already reserved as E820_RESERVED from 0x9fc00 to 0xe0000.

The pin count now has one definition, in arch, which both the MP table and
the IOAPIC emulation read, because the two disagreeing is precisely the bug
this fixes. All three backends stay correct: the WHP and KVM split-irqchip
userspace IOAPIC already implemented 24 pins and bounds-check against the
same constant; KVM's in-kernel IOAPIC has KVM_IOAPIC_NUM_PINS == 24 and
default routing for GSI 0-23; hvf and the aarch64/riscv64 layouts are
untouched, each carrying its own IRQ_BASE/IRQ_MAX.

This raises the budget from 11 devices to 19. It does not make the per-layer
device count scale -- a 20-layer image would still exhaust 19 lines -- but
that is a design question for the snapshotter, not the interrupt map.

Verified by cross-compilation and cross-execution on an aarch64 Linux host:
`cargo check` is clean for x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu
and x86_64-pc-windows-msvc with --features blk,net; krun.dll links and still
exports all 20 symbols nerdbox binds; the new mptable test runs under
qemu-x86_64 and asserts every pin 0-23 has exactly one mp_INT entry. Not
verified: no VM was booted. The claim that the guest now accepts IRQ 16-23
rests on the Linux sources cited above, not on execution.
---
 src/arch/src/x86_64/layout.rs    | 32 +++++++++++-
 src/arch/src/x86_64/mptable.rs   | 89 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 src/devices/src/legacy/ioapic.rs |  8 ++-
 3 files changed, 123 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/arch/src/x86_64/layout.rs b/src/arch/src/x86_64/layout.rs
index a52285d..0336602 100644
--- a/src/arch/src/x86_64/layout.rs
+++ b/src/arch/src/x86_64/layout.rs
@@ -22,11 +22,39 @@ pub const INITRD_SEV_START: u64 = 0xa00000;
 /// Start of the high memory.
 pub const HIMEM_START: u64 = 0x0010_0000; //1 MB.
 
-// Typically, on x86 systems 16 IRQs are used (0-15).
+/// Number of redirection entries (pins) on the single emulated IOAPIC.
+///
+/// 24 is the count every IOAPIC since the 82093AA has had, and the one the
+/// emulation in `devices::legacy::ioapic` implements: it sizes `ioredtbl` to
+/// this and reports `IOAPIC_NUM_PINS - 1` in the max-redirection-entry field
+/// of the version register, which is what the guest reads back. KVM's
+/// in-kernel IOAPIC agrees (`KVM_IOAPIC_NUM_PINS == 24`).
+///
+/// Linux drives its pin loop straight off that version register
+/// (`io_apic_get_redir_entries()` -> `ioapics[].nr_registers` ->
+/// `for_each_ioapic_pin`), so all 24 pins are walked at boot regardless of
+/// what we do here. Whether a pin is *usable* is decided by the MP table:
+/// `setup_IO_APIC_irqs()` calls `find_irq_entry()` per pin and only allocates
+/// an IRQ for pins that have an `mp_INT` source entry. See `mptable.rs`,
+/// which emits one entry per pin for exactly this reason.
+pub const IOAPIC_NUM_PINS: u32 = 24;
+
+// Pins 0-4 are spoken for by the legacy ISA devices this VMM still builds:
+// 0 is the PIT, 1 the i8042 keyboard, 2 the 8259 cascade, and 3/4 the four
+// 16550 serial ports (COM2/COM4 on 3, COM1/COM3 on 4) -- see the
+// `register_irqfd_evt!` calls in `vmm::builder`. Nothing claims a pin above
+// 4: there is no PCI bus in this VMM (so the PIRQ lines 16-23 have no other
+// user) and no ACPI at all (so there is no SCI to reserve either).
 /// First usable IRQ ID for virtio device interrupts on x86_64.
 pub const IRQ_BASE: u32 = 5;
 /// Last usable IRQ ID for virtio device interrupts on x86_64.
-pub const IRQ_MAX: u32 = 15;
+///
+/// One virtio-mmio device is given one IOAPIC pin, and the guest is told which
+/// one through `virtio_mmio.device=<size>@<addr>:<irq>` on the kernel command
+/// line. On x86_64 Linux maps that number 1:1 onto the GSI, and with a single
+/// IOAPIC at GSI base 0 the GSI is the pin, so the last usable pin is the last
+/// usable IRQ.
+pub const IRQ_MAX: u32 = IOAPIC_NUM_PINS - 1;
 
 /// Address for the TSS setup.
 pub const KVM_TSS_ADDRESS: u64 = 0xfffb_d000;
diff --git a/src/arch/src/x86_64/mptable.rs b/src/arch/src/x86_64/mptable.rs
index dcc2273..bee31e8 100644
--- a/src/arch/src/x86_64/mptable.rs
+++ b/src/arch/src/x86_64/mptable.rs
@@ -14,6 +14,8 @@ use libc::c_char;
 use arch_gen::x86::mpspec;
 use vm_memory::{Address, ByteValued, Bytes, GuestAddress, GuestMemory, GuestMemoryMmap};
 
+use super::layout::IOAPIC_NUM_PINS;
+
 // This is a workaround to the Rust enforcement specifying that any implementation of a foreign
 // trait (in this case `ByteValued`) where:
 // *    the type that is implementing the trait is foreign or
@@ -119,7 +121,7 @@ fn compute_mp_size(num_cpus: u8) -> usize {
         + mem::size_of::<MpcCpuWrapper>() * (num_cpus as usize)
         + mem::size_of::<MpcIoapicWrapper>()
         + mem::size_of::<MpcBusWrapper>()
-        + mem::size_of::<MpcIntsrcWrapper>() * 16
+        + mem::size_of::<MpcIntsrcWrapper>() * IOAPIC_NUM_PINS as usize
         + mem::size_of::<MpcLintsrcWrapper>() * 2
 }
 
@@ -214,8 +216,24 @@ pub fn setup_mptable(mem: &GuestMemoryMmap, num_cpus: u8) -> Result<()> {
         base_mp = base_mp.unchecked_add(size);
         checksum = checksum.wrapping_add(compute_checksum(&mpc_ioapic.0));
     }
-    // Per kvm_setup_default_irq_routing() in kernel
-    for i in 0..16 {
+    // One mp_INT source entry per IOAPIC pin, not just per legacy ISA IRQ.
+    //
+    // This is what actually decides how many IRQs a guest can use. Linux walks
+    // every pin the version register advertises, but in `setup_IO_APIC_irqs()`
+    // it only allocates an IRQ for a pin that `find_irq_entry(.., mp_INT)`
+    // finds an entry for; a pin without one is logged "not connected" and left
+    // with no irq_desc, so a `virtio_mmio.device=...:<pin>` naming it would
+    // fail at request_irq() even though the pin exists in the IOAPIC. Stopping
+    // at 16 therefore capped virtio at IRQ 15 no matter what IRQ_MAX said.
+    //
+    // Declaring 16-23 here is safe because we own the whole interrupt map:
+    // there is no PCI bus for these pins to collide with (on real hardware
+    // they are PIRQ A-H) and no ACPI to describe them differently. On x86_64
+    // `pin_2_irq()` ignores srcbusirq entirely -- the PIRQ override path that
+    // reads it is CONFIG_X86_32-only -- and `alloc_irq_from_domain()` hands
+    // back irq == gsi == pin for gsi >= nr_legacy_irqs(), so pin 16 really is
+    // IRQ 16 to the guest.
+    for i in 0..IOAPIC_NUM_PINS as u8 {
         let size = mem::size_of::<MpcIntsrcWrapper>() as u64;
         let mut mpc_intsrc = MpcIntsrcWrapper(mpspec::mpc_intsrc::default());
         mpc_intsrc.0.type_ = mpspec::MP_INTSRC as u8;
@@ -301,6 +319,71 @@ mod tests {
         }
     }
 
+    /// Every IOAPIC pin must have an `mp_INT` source entry, because that is
+    /// what decides whether the guest can use it.
+    ///
+    /// Linux walks pins 0..`nr_registers` (24, read back from our version
+    /// register) and, per pin, looks for an entry with `dstirq == pin`; a pin
+    /// without one is logged "not connected" and never gets an irq_desc, so a
+    /// `virtio_mmio.device=...:<pin>` naming it cannot be requested. Declaring
+    /// only 0..16 was therefore the real ceiling on device count -- IRQ_MAX
+    /// could say 23 and the guest would still refuse pins 16-23.
+    ///
+    /// This asserts the full range is covered exactly once, so that IRQ_BASE
+    /// ..=IRQ_MAX is backed end to end.
+    #[test]
+    fn intsrc_entry_per_ioapic_pin() {
+        let num_cpus = 4;
+        let mem = GuestMemoryMmap::from_ranges(&[(
+            GuestAddress(MPTABLE_START),
+            compute_mp_size(num_cpus),
+        )])
+        .unwrap();
+        setup_mptable(&mem, num_cpus).unwrap();
+
+        let mpf_intel: MpfIntelWrapper = mem.read_obj(GuestAddress(MPTABLE_START)).unwrap();
+        let mpc_offset = GuestAddress(u64::from(mpf_intel.0.physptr));
+        let mpc_table: MpcTableWrapper = mem.read_obj(mpc_offset).unwrap();
+        let mpc_end = mpc_offset
+            .checked_add(u64::from(mpc_table.0.length))
+            .unwrap();
+
+        let mut entry_offset = mpc_offset
+            .checked_add(mem::size_of::<MpcTableWrapper>() as u64)
+            .unwrap();
+        let mut seen = vec![0u32; IOAPIC_NUM_PINS as usize];
+        while entry_offset < mpc_end {
+            let entry_type: u8 = mem.read_obj(entry_offset).unwrap();
+            if u32::from(entry_type) == mpspec::MP_INTSRC {
+                let intsrc: MpcIntsrcWrapper = mem.read_obj(entry_offset).unwrap();
+                assert_eq!(
+                    u32::from(intsrc.0.irqtype),
+                    mpspec::mp_irq_source_types_mp_INT,
+                    "interrupt source entries must be mp_INT for find_irq_entry() to match"
+                );
+                let pin = intsrc.0.dstirq;
+                assert!(
+                    (pin as u32) < IOAPIC_NUM_PINS,
+                    "entry targets pin {pin}, which the IOAPIC does not have"
+                );
+                seen[pin as usize] += 1;
+            }
+            entry_offset = entry_offset
+                .checked_add(table_entry_size(entry_type) as u64)
+                .unwrap();
+            assert!(entry_offset <= mpc_end);
+        }
+
+        for (pin, count) in seen.iter().enumerate() {
+            assert_eq!(*count, 1, "IOAPIC pin {pin} has {count} mp_INT entries");
+        }
+
+        // The interrupts the MMIO device manager will hand out must all be
+        // inside the range just proven to be declared.
+        assert!(super::super::layout::IRQ_BASE < super::super::layout::IRQ_MAX);
+        assert_eq!(super::super::layout::IRQ_MAX, IOAPIC_NUM_PINS - 1);
+    }
+
     #[test]
     fn bounds_check() {
         let num_cpus = 4;
diff --git a/src/devices/src/legacy/ioapic.rs b/src/devices/src/legacy/ioapic.rs
index 113db46..bc462e6 100644
--- a/src/devices/src/legacy/ioapic.rs
+++ b/src/devices/src/legacy/ioapic.rs
@@ -13,7 +13,13 @@ use crate::bus::BusDevice;
 use crate::legacy::irqchip::IrqChipT;
 
 const IOAPIC_BASE: u32 = 0xfec0_0000;
-pub(super) const IOAPIC_NUM_PINS: usize = 24;
+
+/// Taken from `arch` rather than restated, because the MP table has to declare
+/// an `mp_INT` source entry for every pin the version register advertises here
+/// -- a pin the guest sees but finds no entry for is dead weight, and a pin the
+/// MP table declares but this emulation does not have would be worse. Keeping
+/// one definition makes the two impossible to disagree.
+pub(super) const IOAPIC_NUM_PINS: usize = arch::x86_64::layout::IOAPIC_NUM_PINS as usize;
 
 const IO_REG_SEL: u64 = 0x00;
 const IO_WIN: u64 = 0x10;
